      "text": "Andrew Carnegie was born on November 25, 1835, in Dunfermline, Scotland.",
      "date": "1835-11-25"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Emigration to the United States",
      "text": "In 1848, Andrew Carnegie emigrated with his family from Scotland to the United States, settling in Allegheny, Pennsylvania.",
      "date": "1848"
    },
    {
      "headline": "First Job in a Cotton Factory",
      "text": "At the age of 13, in 1848, Carnegie began working as a bobbin boy in a cotton factory.",
      "date": "1848"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Telegraph Messenger Boy",
      "text": "In 1850, Carnegie became a telegraph messenger boy in Pittsburgh, which was a stepping stone to his later success.",
      "date": "1850"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Promotion to Telegraph Operator",
      "text": "By 1853, Carnegie was promoted to a telegraph operator at the Pennsylvania Railroad Company.",
      "date": "1853"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Superintendent of the Pennsylvania Railroad",
      "text": "In 1859, Carnegie was appointed as the superintendent of the Western Division of the Pennsylvania Railroad.",
      "date": "1859"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Investment in the Keystone Bridge Company",
      "text": "In 1865, Carnegie invested in the Keystone Bridge Company, marking his first major step into the steel industry.",
      "date": "1865"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Formation of Carnegie Steel Company",
      "text": "In 1892, Carnegie formed the Carnegie Steel Company, which revolutionized steel production in the United States.",
      "date": "1892"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Sale of Carnegie Steel Company",
      "text": "In 1901, Carnegie sold his steel company to J.P. Morgan for $480 million, making it the largest business transaction of the time.",
      "date": "1901"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Philanthropic Efforts",
      "text": "Throughout his later life, Carnegie dedicated himself to philanthropy, establishing libraries, universities, and various trusts. Notably, he founded the Carnegie Corporation of New York in 1911.",
      "date": "1911"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Death of Andrew Carnegie",
      "text": "Andrew Carnegie died on August 11, 1919, in Lenox, Massachusetts, at the age of 83.",
      "date": "1919-08-11"
    }
  ]
}

