      "headline": "Creation of Hangul",
      "text": "Hangul was created during the 15th century by King Sejong the Great and his scholars. The exact year of its creation is 1443.",
      "date": "1443"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Proclamation of Hangul",
      "text": "Hangul was officially proclaimed in 1446. It was initially called 'Hunminjeongeum', which means 'The Correct Sounds for the Instruction of the People'.",
      "date": "1446"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Publication of Hunminjeongeum Haerye",
      "text": "In 1446, the document 'Hunminjeongeum Haerye' was published, explaining the principles and usage of Hangul.",
      "date": "1446"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Suppression of Hangul",
      "text": "During the late 15th century, Hangul faced suppression by the ruling elite who preferred Classical Chinese for official documents.",
      "date": "1490"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Revival of Hangul",
      "text": "In the late 19th century, Hangul experienced a revival as part of a movement to promote Korean nationalism and identity.",
      "date": "1894"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Hangul Day Established",
      "text": "Hangul Day was established in 1926 to commemorate the creation of the Korean alphabet.",
      "date": "1926"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Official Script of Korea",
      "text": "After Korea's liberation from Japanese rule in 1945, Hangul was established as the official script of Korea.",
      "date": "1945"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Hangul in North and South Korea",
      "text": "Following the division of Korea in 1948, Hangul continued to be used in both North and South Korea, with some variations in orthography.",
      "date": "1948"
    }
  ]
}

