      "headline": "Split from the RSDLP",
      "text": "In 1903, during the Second Congress of the Russian Social Democratic Labour Party (RSDLP), the party split into two factions: the Bolsheviks, led by Vladimir Lenin, and the Mensheviks. This division was based on differing views on party membership and organization.",
      "date": "1903"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Formal Establishment of the Bolshevik Party",
      "text": "In 1912, the Bolsheviks formally established themselves as a separate party, distinct from the Mensheviks, during a conference in Prague.",
      "date": "1912"
    },
    {
      "headline": "October Revolution",
      "text": "In October 1917, the Bolsheviks, led by Lenin, seized power in Russia during the October Revolution. This marked the beginning of Bolshevik control over Russia.",
      "date": "1917-10"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Renaming to Russian Communist Party",
      "text": "In 1918, the Bolshevik Party was renamed the Russian Communist Party (Bolsheviks), reflecting its commitment to communist ideology.",
      "date": "1918"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Formation of the Soviet Union",
      "text": "In 1922, the Bolsheviks played a key role in the formation of the Union of Soviet Socialist Republics (USSR), consolidating their power over the newly established state.",
      "date": "1922"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Renaming to All-Union Communist Party",
      "text": "In 1925, the party was renamed the All-Union Communist Party (Bolsheviks) to reflect its authority over the entire Soviet Union.",
      "date": "1925"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Renaming to Communist Party of the Soviet Union",
      "text": "In 1952, the party was renamed the Communist Party of the Soviet Union (CPSU), which it remained until the dissolution of the Soviet Union.",
      "date": "1952"
    }
  ]
}

