      "headline": "Development of Pegasus",
      "text": "Pegasus was developed by the Israeli cyber-arms company NSO Group. The exact date of its initial development is not publicly specified, but it is known to have been operational by the early 2010s.",
      "date": "2010"
    },
    {
      "headline": "First Public Exposure",
      "text": "In August 2016, Pegasus was publicly exposed after an attempted attack on human rights activist Ahmed Mansoor was discovered. Researchers at Citizen Lab and Lookout Security analyzed the spyware and revealed its capabilities.",
      "date": "2016-08"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Apple Security Update",
      "text": "Following the exposure of Pegasus, Apple released a security update (iOS 9.3.5) to patch the vulnerabilities exploited by the spyware.",
      "date": "2016-08-25"
    },
    {
      "headline": "WhatsApp Lawsuit",
      "text": "In October 2019, WhatsApp sued NSO Group, alleging that Pegasus was used to hack into the phones of 1,400 users, including journalists and human rights activists.",
      "date": "2019-10"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Pegasus Project Revelations",
      "text": "In July 2021, a collaborative investigation by 17 media organizations, known as the Pegasus Project, revealed that Pegasus had been used to target thousands of individuals, including journalists, politicians, and activists worldwide.",
      "date": "2021-07"
    },
    {
      "headline": "U.S. Blacklists NSO Group",
      "text": "In November 2021, the United States Department of Commerce added NSO Group to its Entity List, effectively blacklisting the company and restricting its access to American technology.",
      "date": "2021-11"
    }
  ]
}

