      "headline": "First Council of Nicaea",
      "text": "The Nicene Creed was first formulated at the First Council of Nicaea in 325 AD. This ecumenical council was convened by Emperor Constantine I to address the Arian controversy and establish a unified Christian doctrine.",
      "date": "0325"
    },
    {
      "headline": "First Council of Constantinople",
      "text": "In 381 AD, the First Council of Constantinople revised the Nicene Creed to address further theological disputes and to affirm the divinity of the Holy Spirit. This version is often referred to as the Niceno-Constantinopolitan Creed.",
      "date": "0381"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Council of Chalcedon",
      "text": "The Council of Chalcedon in 451 AD reaffirmed the Nicene Creed and further clarified the nature of Christ, emphasizing the creed's importance in defining orthodox Christian beliefs.",
      "date": "0451"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Third Council of Toledo",
      "text": "In 589 AD, the Third Council of Toledo in Spain added the Filioque clause to the Nicene Creed, stating that the Holy Spirit proceeds from the Father and the Son. This addition later became a point of contention between the Eastern and Western Churches.",
      "date": "0589"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Great Schism",
      "text": "The Great Schism of 1054 AD was partly influenced by disagreements over the Filioque clause in the Nicene Creed, contributing to the division between the Roman Catholic and Eastern Orthodox Churches.",
      "date": "1054"
    }
  ]
}

