      "text": "The Badge of Military Merit, the precursor to the Purple Heart, was established by General George Washington on August 7, 1782. It was awarded to soldiers for 'any singularly meritorious action' and took the form of a heart made of purple cloth.",
      "date": "1782-08-07"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Revival of the Purple Heart",
      "text": "The Purple Heart was revived on February 22, 1932, the 200th anniversary of George Washington's birth. It was reintroduced by General Douglas MacArthur and was awarded to soldiers who were wounded or killed in action.",
      "date": "1932-02-22"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Eligibility Extended to All Services",
      "text": "In 1942, the eligibility for the Purple Heart was extended to all branches of the U.S. military, allowing members of the Navy, Marine Corps, and Coast Guard to receive the award.",
      "date": "1942"
    },
    {
      "headline": "National Purple Heart Hall of Honor Opens",
      "text": "The National Purple Heart Hall of Honor was opened in New Windsor, New York, in November 2006. It serves as a museum and memorial to honor the recipients of the Purple Heart.",
      "date": "2006-11"
    }
  ]
}

