      "headline": "Birth of Hachikō",
      "text": "Hachikō was born on November 10, 1923, in a farm near the city of Ōdate, Akita Prefecture, Japan.",
      "date": "1923-11-10"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Adoption by Hidesaburō Ueno",
      "text": "In 1924, Hachikō was adopted by Hidesaburō Ueno, a professor at the University of Tokyo, and moved to live with him in Shibuya, Tokyo.",
      "date": "1924"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Death of Hidesaburō Ueno",
      "text": "On May 21, 1925, Hidesaburō Ueno died suddenly from a cerebral hemorrhage while at work, never returning to the Shibuya Station where Hachikō waited for him.",
      "date": "1925-05-21"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Hachikō's Daily Vigil",
      "text": "After Ueno's death, Hachikō continued to wait for his owner at Shibuya Station every day, appearing precisely when the train was due at the station, for over nine years.",
      "date": "1925-1935"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Recognition and Fame",
      "text": "In 1932, an article about Hachikō's remarkable loyalty was published in Asahi Shimbun, making him a national sensation in Japan.",
      "date": "1932"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Death of Hachikō",
      "text": "Hachikō died on March 8, 1935, on a street in Shibuya, Tokyo. His remains were preserved and are on display at the National Science Museum of Japan in Ueno, Tokyo.",
      "date": "1935-03-08"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Hachikō Statue Unveiling",
      "text": "In April 1934, a bronze statue in honor of Hachikō was erected at Shibuya Station, with Hachikō himself present at the unveiling ceremony.",
      "date": "1934-04"
    }
  ]
}

