      "headline": "Foundation of Lahore",
      "text": "Lahore's origins are shrouded in legend, with some traditions tracing its founding to the mythological figure Lava, son of the Hindu god Rama. The earliest recorded mention of Lahore is in the 982 CE book 'Hudud al-'Alam' where it is referred to as a small city with impressive temples.",
      "date": "0982"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Lahore under Ghaznavid Rule",
      "text": "In the 11th century, Lahore became a part of the Ghaznavid Empire. It was made the empire's eastern capital by Sultan Mahmud of Ghazni in 1021.",
      "date": "1021"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Ghurid Conquest",
      "text": "In 1186, Lahore was captured by Muhammad of Ghor, marking the end of Ghaznavid rule and the beginning of the Ghurid Empire's control over the city.",
      "date": "1186"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Mughal Era Begins",
      "text": "Lahore became a major cultural and architectural center during the Mughal Empire, starting with the reign of Emperor Akbar in 1584, who made it his capital.",
      "date": "1584"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Construction of Lahore Fort",
      "text": "The Lahore Fort, a UNESCO World Heritage Site, was extensively rebuilt and expanded under the Mughal Emperor Akbar in 1566.",
      "date": "1566"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Sikh Empire",
      "text": "In 1799, Lahore was captured by Ranjit Singh, marking the beginning of the Sikh Empire's rule over the city.",
      "date": "1799"
    },
    {
      "headline": "British Colonial Rule",
      "text": "Following the defeat of the Sikh Empire in the Second Anglo-Sikh War, Lahore came under British control in 1849.",
      "date": "1849"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Lahore Resolution",
      "text": "On March 23, 1940, the All-India Muslim League passed the Lahore Resolution, which called for the creation of independent states for Muslims in northwestern and eastern British India.",
      "date": "1940-03-23"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Partition of India",
      "text": "In 1947, Lahore became a part of Pakistan following the partition of British India, which led to significant demographic changes in the city.",
      "date": "1947"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Lahore as Capital of Punjab",
      "text": "After the independence of Pakistan in 1947, Lahore was declared the capital of the Punjab province.",
      "date": "1947"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Lahore Declaration",
      "text": "On February 21, 1999, the Lahore Declaration was signed by India and Pakistan, aiming to de-escalate nuclear tensions between the two countries.",
