      "text": "In 1950, John Nash introduced the concept of Nash Equilibrium, a fundamental principle in non-cooperative games, which describes a situation where no player can benefit by changing strategies while the other players keep theirs unchanged.",
      "date": "1950"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Development of the Prisoner's Dilemma",
      "text": "In 1950, Merrill Flood and Melvin Dresher developed the Prisoner's Dilemma, a standard example of a game analyzed in game theory that shows why two completely rational individuals might not cooperate, even if it appears that it is in their best interest to do so.",
      "date": "1950"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Publication of 'The Strategy of Conflict'",
      "text": "In 1960, Thomas Schelling published 'The Strategy of Conflict', which introduced concepts such as focal points and the importance of credible commitments in strategic games.",
      "date": "1960"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Nobel Prize in Economics for Game Theory",
      "text": "In 1994, John Nash, John Harsanyi, and Reinhard Selten were awarded the Nobel Prize in Economics for their pioneering analysis of equilibria in the theory of non-cooperative games.",
      "date": "1994"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Nobel Prize for Mechanism Design Theory",
      "text": "In 2007, Leonid Hurwicz, Eric Maskin, and Roger Myerson were awarded the Nobel Prize in Economics for having laid the foundations of mechanism design theory, a branch of game theory.",
      "date": "2007"
    }
  ]
}

{
