    {
      "headline": "Development of Infinitesimal Calculus",
      "text": "In the late 17th century, Isaac Newton and Gottfried Wilhelm Leibniz independently developed the foundations of infinitesimal calculus. Newton focused on the concept of fluxions, while Leibniz developed the notation that is still in use today.",
      "date": "1670"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Publication of 'Principia Mathematica'",
      "text": "In 1687, Isaac Newton published 'Philosophiæ Naturalis Principia Mathematica', which laid the groundwork for classical mechanics and included his laws of motion and universal gravitation, using calculus as a tool.",
      "date": "1687"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Leibniz's Publication on Calculus",
      "text": "In 1684, Leibniz published his first paper on differential calculus, 'Nova Methodus pro Maximis et Minimis', which introduced his notation and methods for calculus.",
      "date": "1684"
    },
    {
      "headline": "The Calculus Controversy",
      "text": "The calculus controversy was a major intellectual conflict between followers of Newton and Leibniz over who invented calculus first. This dispute lasted from the late 17th century into the 18th century.",
      "date": "1699"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Formalization of Calculus",
      "text": "In the 19th century, mathematicians such as Augustin-Louis Cauchy, Bernhard Riemann, and Karl Weierstrass worked on rigorously formalizing calculus, addressing issues of limits, continuity, and convergence.",
      "date": "1800"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Introduction of Non-Standard Analysis",
      "text": "In 1960, Abraham Robinson introduced non-standard analysis, a mathematical framework that rigorously formalizes the notion of infinitesimals, providing an alternative foundation for calculus.",
      "date": "1960"
    }
  ]
}

