      "headline": "Origin of the Phrase",
      "text": "The phrase 'Let's Go Brandon' originated on October 2, 2021, during a televised interview with NASCAR driver Brandon Brown after his victory at the Talladega Superspeedway. The crowd was chanting 'Fuck Joe Biden,' but the NBC reporter mistakenly or intentionally suggested they were chanting 'Let's Go Brandon.'",
      "date": "2021-10-02"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Viral Spread",
      "text": "Following the interview, 'Let's Go Brandon' quickly became a viral meme and slogan among critics of President Joe Biden. It was widely shared on social media and used in various forms of media and merchandise.",
      "date": "2021-10"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Political Adoption",
      "text": "The slogan was adopted by several Republican politicians and public figures as a way to express disapproval of President Biden without using explicit language. It was used in speeches, social media posts, and campaign materials.",
      "date": "2021-10"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Cultural Impact",
      "text": "The phrase 'Let's Go Brandon' became a cultural phenomenon, appearing on T-shirts, hats, and other merchandise. It was also referenced in songs and other forms of entertainment.",
      "date": "2021-11"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Commercialization",
      "text": "Various companies and individuals began selling 'Let's Go Brandon' merchandise, capitalizing on the slogan's popularity. This included clothing, flags, and other items.",
      "date": "2021-11"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Continued Usage",
      "text": "The phrase continued to be used throughout 2022 as a political statement and meme, maintaining its presence in American political discourse.",
      "date": "2022"
    }
  ]
}

