      "headline": "Laying of the Foundation Stone",
      "text": "The foundation stone for Neuschwanstein Castle was laid on September 5, 1869. This marked the beginning of the construction of the castle, which was commissioned by Ludwig II of Bavaria.",
      "date": "1869-09-05"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Topping Out Ceremony",
      "text": "The topping out ceremony for Neuschwanstein Castle took place in 1880. By this time, the structure of the castle was largely completed, although interior work continued.",
      "date": "1880"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Death of King Ludwig II",
      "text": "King Ludwig II of Bavaria died under mysterious circumstances on June 13, 1886. His death occurred before the completion of Neuschwanstein Castle, which he had envisioned as a personal refuge.",
      "date": "1886-06-13"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Opening to the Public",
      "text": "Neuschwanstein Castle was opened to the public shortly after the death of King Ludwig II in 1886. Despite being incomplete, it quickly became a popular tourist attraction.",
      "date": "1886"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Completion of the Castle",
      "text": "Although Neuschwanstein Castle was never fully completed as originally planned, construction continued until 1892. The castle remains unfinished in some parts.",
      "date": "1892"
    },
    {
      "headline": "World War II and the Castle",
      "text": "During World War II, Neuschwanstein Castle was used by the Nazis to store looted art. The castle was not damaged during the war and was later used by the Allies to catalog the recovered art.",
      "date": "1940s"
    },
    {
      "headline": "UNESCO World Heritage Tentative List",
      "text": "In 2015, Neuschwanstein Castle was added to the UNESCO World Heritage Tentative List as part of a proposal to recognize the cultural significance of Ludwig II's castles.",
      "date": "2015"
    }
  ]
}

