      "headline": "Beginning of Moai Construction",
      "text": "The construction of the Moai statues by the Rapa Nui people began around 1250 CE. These monolithic human figures were carved on Rapa Nui, also known as Easter Island, in eastern Polynesia.",
      "date": "1250"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Peak of Moai Construction",
      "text": "The peak period of Moai construction occurred between 1400 and 1500 CE. During this time, hundreds of Moai were carved and transported to stone platforms called ahu around the island's perimeter.",
      "date": "1400"
    },
    {
      "headline": "End of Moai Construction",
      "text": "The construction of Moai statues largely ceased by 1500 CE. Nearly half of the Moai remain at Rano Raraku, the main quarry, while others were moved to various locations on the island.",
      "date": "1500"
    },
    {
      "headline": "European Discovery of Easter Island",
      "text": "Easter Island was discovered by Europeans on April 5, 1722, when Dutch explorer Jacob Roggeveen arrived. The Moai statues were observed by the Europeans during this time.",
      "date": "1722-04-05"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Collapse of Moai Culture",
      "text": "By the late 19th century, the Moai culture had collapsed due to various factors, including deforestation, resource depletion, and European contact, which led to the toppling of many Moai statues.",
      "date": "1800"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Restoration Efforts Begin",
      "text": "In the 20th century, efforts to restore and preserve the Moai statues began. This included re-erecting toppled statues and conserving those that remained standing.",
      "date": "1900"
    }
  ]
}

