      "headline": "Establishment of Artemis Program",
      "text": "The Artemis program was formally established in 2017 via Space Policy Directive 1. This directive was signed by President Donald Trump, directing NASA to lead an innovative and sustainable program of exploration with commercial and international partners to enable human expansion across the solar system.",
      "date": "2017"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Artemis Program Announcement",
      "text": "NASA officially announced the Artemis program in May 2019, naming it after the Greek goddess of the Moon and twin sister of Apollo, symbolizing the program's goal to return humans to the Moon.",
      "date": "2019-05"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Artemis I Mission",
      "text": "Artemis I, an uncrewed test flight of the Space Launch System (SLS) and the Orion spacecraft, was initially planned for 2020 but faced several delays. It is intended to demonstrate NASA's new deep space exploration systems.",
      "date": "2020"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Artemis II Mission",
      "text": "Artemis II is planned to be the first crewed mission of the Artemis program, aiming to carry astronauts around the Moon and back to Earth. It is scheduled to follow Artemis I.",
      "date": "2024"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Artemis III Mission",
      "text": "Artemis III is planned to land 'the first woman and the next man' on the Moon by 2025. This mission aims to land astronauts near the lunar South Pole, where they will conduct scientific research and exploration.",
      "date": "2025"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Long-term Goals of Artemis Program",
      "text": "The long-term goal of the Artemis program is to establish a sustainable human presence on the Moon by the end of the decade and to use this as a stepping stone for future human missions to Mars.",
      "date": "2030"
    }
  ]
}

