      "headline": "Beginning of Comfort Women System",
      "text": "The system of 'comfort women' began in the 1930s, with the Imperial Japanese Armed Forces forcing women and girls into sexual slavery in occupied territories.",
      "date": "1930"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Expansion During World War II",
      "text": "During World War II, the practice expanded significantly, with hundreds of thousands of women from various countries, primarily Korea, forced into sexual slavery for Japanese troops.",
      "date": "1939"
    },
    {
      "headline": "End of World War II",
      "text": "World War II ended in 1945, but the issue of comfort women was largely ignored in the immediate post-war period.",
      "date": "1945"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Initial Denial by Japan",
      "text": "After the war, Japan denied the existence of comfort women, refusing to provide an apology or restitution, which damaged its reputation in Asia.",
      "date": "1945"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Revelation of Official Records",
      "text": "In the 1990s, official records were revealed showing the Japanese government's culpability in the comfort women system.",
      "date": "1990"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Japanese Government Apologies",
      "text": "In the 1990s, the Japanese government began to offer official apologies and compensation to former comfort women.",
      "date": "1990"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Criticism of Apologies",
      "text": "Apologies from Japanese officials have been criticized as insincere, and some Japanese government officials have continued to deny the existence of comfort women.",
      "date": "1990"
    }
  ]
}

