      "headline": "Identification of Salmonella Typhi",
      "text": "In 1880, Karl Joseph Eberth identified the bacterium Salmonella enterica serotype Typhi as the cause of typhoid fever.",
      "date": "1880"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Development of the First Typhoid Vaccine",
      "text": "In 1896, Almroth Wright developed the first effective typhoid vaccine, which was used to protect British soldiers during the Boer War.",
      "date": "1896"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Mary Mallon (Typhoid Mary) Identified as a Carrier",
      "text": "In 1907, Mary Mallon, known as Typhoid Mary, was identified as the first asymptomatic carrier of typhoid fever in the United States. She was forcibly isolated to prevent further spread of the disease.",
      "date": "1907"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Introduction of Chloramphenicol",
      "text": "In 1948, chloramphenicol was introduced as the first antibiotic effective against typhoid fever, significantly reducing mortality rates.",
      "date": "1948"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Emergence of Antibiotic Resistance",
      "text": "In the 1970s, strains of Salmonella Typhi resistant to chloramphenicol and other antibiotics began to emerge, complicating treatment efforts.",
      "date": "1970"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Development of Oral Typhoid Vaccine",
      "text": "In 1989, an oral typhoid vaccine was developed, providing a more convenient option for immunization against the disease.",
      "date": "1989"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Introduction of Vi Polysaccharide Vaccine",
      "text": "In 1994, the Vi polysaccharide vaccine was introduced, offering another effective method for preventing typhoid fever.",
      "date": "1994"
    }
  ]
}

