      "headline": "Introduction of Renminbi",
      "text": "The renminbi was introduced by the People's Bank of China in December 1948, shortly before the establishment of the People's Republic of China.",
      "date": "1948-12"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Establishment of the People's Republic of China",
      "text": "On October 1, 1949, the People's Republic of China was established, and the renminbi became the official currency.",
      "date": "1949-10-01"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Currency Reforms",
      "text": "In 1955, the first major currency reform took place, replacing the old renminbi with a new version at a rate of 1 new yuan = 10,000 old yuan.",
      "date": "1955"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Introduction of the Yuan as a Unit",
      "text": "In 1969, the yuan was officially introduced as the basic unit of the renminbi, with jiao and fen as subunits.",
      "date": "1969"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Economic Reforms and Opening Up",
      "text": "In 1978, China began economic reforms and opening up, leading to increased international trade and the gradual internationalization of the renminbi.",
      "date": "1978"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Renminbi Becomes Convertible for Current Account Transactions",
      "text": "In 1996, the renminbi became convertible for current account transactions, marking a significant step towards internationalization.",
      "date": "1996"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Renminbi Joins IMF's SDR Basket",
      "text": "On October 1, 2016, the renminbi was officially included in the International Monetary Fund's Special Drawing Rights basket, recognizing its importance in the global economy.",
      "date": "2016-10-01"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Renminbi as the World's Fifth-Most-Traded Currency",
      "text": "As of April 2022, the renminbi was the world's fifth-most-traded currency, reflecting its growing role in international finance.",
      "date": "2022-04"
    }
  ]
}

