      "text": "Around 122 AD, the Romans established the fort of Pons Aelius on the site of what is now Newcastle upon Tyne.",
      "date": "0122"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Norman Castle Built",
      "text": "In 1080, Robert Curthose, the eldest son of William the Conqueror, built a wooden castle on the site of the Roman fort, giving the city its name, Newcastle.",
      "date": "1080"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Newcastle Receives Town Charter",
      "text": "In 1175, Newcastle was granted a town charter by King Henry II, marking its official recognition as a town.",
      "date": "1175"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Newcastle Becomes a County",
      "text": "In 1400, Newcastle was granted the status of a county corporate, giving it administrative independence from Northumberland.",
      "date": "1400"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Great Fire of Newcastle and Gateshead",
      "text": "On October 6, 1854, a major fire broke out in Newcastle and Gateshead, causing extensive damage and loss of life.",
      "date": "1854-10-06"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Tyne Bridge Opens",
      "text": "The Tyne Bridge, a significant architectural and engineering landmark, was officially opened on October 10, 1928.",
      "date": "1928-10-10"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Newcastle University Established",
      "text": "In 1963, Newcastle University was established as an independent university, having previously been part of the University of Durham.",
      "date": "1963"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Metro Centre Opens",
      "text": "On October 14, 1986, the MetroCentre, one of the largest shopping centers in Europe, opened in nearby Gateshead, impacting Newcastle's economy.",
      "date": "1986-10-14"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Newcastle Becomes a Unitary Authority",
      "text": "In 1997, Newcastle upon Tyne became a unitary authority, gaining greater control over its local governance.",
