      "headline": "Invention of the Scoville Scale",
      "text": "The Scoville scale was invented by American pharmacist Wilbur Scoville in 1912. It measures the pungency (spicy heat) of chili peppers and other spicy foods.",
      "date": "1912"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Publication of the Scoville Organoleptic Test",
      "text": "Wilbur Scoville published the Scoville Organoleptic Test in 1912, which was the first method to measure the heat level of peppers. This test involved diluting a pepper extract in sugar water until its heat was no longer detectable to a panel of tasters.",
      "date": "1912"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Development of High-Performance Liquid Chromatography (HPLC)",
      "text": "In the late 20th century, High-Performance Liquid Chromatography (HPLC) became a more accurate method for measuring the capsaicinoid content of peppers, providing a more precise measurement of heat than the original Scoville Organoleptic Test.",
      "date": "1980"
    }
  ]
}

