      "text": "The Great Purge officially began in 1936. It was initiated by Joseph Stalin to consolidate his power and eliminate any remaining influence of Leon Trotsky within the Soviet Union.",
      "date": "1936"
    },
    {
      "headline": "First Moscow Trial",
      "text": "The first Moscow Trial, also known as the Trial of the Sixteen, took place in August 1936. It was the first of the show trials against former Bolshevik leaders, including Grigory Zinoviev and Lev Kamenev, who were accused of conspiring with Trotsky.",
      "date": "1936-08"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Second Moscow Trial",
      "text": "The second Moscow Trial, also known as the Trial of the Seventeen, occurred in January 1937. It targeted more former Bolshevik leaders, including Karl Radek and Grigory Sokolnikov, who were accused of plotting against the Soviet state.",
      "date": "1937-01"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Military Purge",
      "text": "In June 1937, a significant purge of the Red Army leadership began, resulting in the execution of Marshal Mikhail Tukhachevsky and several other high-ranking military officers.",
      "date": "1937-06"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Third Moscow Trial",
      "text": "The third Moscow Trial, known as the Trial of the Twenty-One, took place in March 1938. It included prominent figures such as Nikolai Bukharin and Alexei Rykov, who were accused of espionage and plotting to overthrow the Soviet government.",
      "date": "1938-03"
    },
    {
      "headline": "End of the Great Purge",
      "text": "The Great Purge effectively ended in 1938, following the execution of many of Stalin's perceived enemies and the consolidation of his power within the Soviet Union.",
      "date": "1938"
    }
  ]
}

