    {
      "headline": "Beginning of Severe Isolation",
      "text": "When Genie was approximately 20 months old, her father began keeping her in a locked room, strapping her to a child's toilet or binding her in a crib with her arms and legs immobilized. This marked the beginning of her severe isolation and abuse.",
      "date": "1958-12"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Discovery and Rescue",
      "text": "In November 1970, at the age of 13 years and 7 months, Genie was discovered by Los Angeles County child welfare authorities. Her case became widely known and she was taken into protective custody.",
      "date": "1970-11"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Becomes a Ward of the State",
      "text": "Following her rescue, Genie became a ward of the state of California. This allowed for her to receive care and begin the process of rehabilitation.",
      "date": "1970-11"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Research and Rehabilitation",
      "text": "After her discovery, Genie became the subject of extensive research in linguistics and psychology. Her case provided significant insights into language acquisition and the effects of social isolation.",
      "date": "1970-1975"
    },
    {
      "headline": "End of Research Studies",
      "text": "By 1975, the research studies on Genie were largely concluded, and she was moved through various foster homes and institutions.",
      "date": "1975"
    }
  ]
}

