      "text": "On May 25, 1521, the Edict of Worms was issued, declaring Martin Luther an outlaw and banning his writings.",
      "date": "1521-05-25"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Peasants' War",
      "text": "From 1524 to 1525, the Peasants' War took place in the Holy Roman Empire, partly inspired by the Reformation's call for reform and equality.",
      "date": "1524"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Augsburg Confession",
      "text": "On June 25, 1530, the Augsburg Confession was presented to Emperor Charles V, outlining the beliefs of the Lutheran Church.",
      "date": "1530-06-25"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Peace of Augsburg",
      "text": "In 1555, the Peace of Augsburg was signed, allowing rulers of the Holy Roman Empire to choose either Lutheranism or Catholicism as the official confession of their state.",
      "date": "1555"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Council of Trent",
      "text": "The Council of Trent was held between 1545 and 1563 as part of the Catholic Counter-Reformation, aiming to address issues raised by the Reformation and reform the Church.",
      "date": "1545"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Thirty Years' War",
      "text": "The Thirty Years' War (1618-1648) was a series of conflicts in Central Europe, rooted in the religious tensions between Protestants and Catholics.",
      "date": "1618"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Peace of Westphalia",
      "text": "In 1648, the Peace of Westphalia treaties ended the Thirty Years' War, marking a turning point in European history by recognizing the sovereignty of states and the coexistence of Protestant and Catholic territories.",
      "date": "1648"
    }
  ]
}

