      "headline": "Birth of Osteopathy",
      "text": "Osteopathy was founded by Andrew Taylor Still in 1874. He developed the practice as a form of alternative medicine that emphasizes physical manipulation of the body's muscle tissue and bones.",
      "date": "1874"
    },
    {
      "headline": "First Osteopathic School",
      "text": "In 1892, Andrew Taylor Still founded the American School of Osteopathy in Kirksville, Missouri, which was the first school dedicated to teaching osteopathy.",
      "date": "1892"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Expansion in the United States",
      "text": "During the late 19th and early 20th centuries, osteopathy began to expand in the United States, with more schools opening and the practice gaining popularity.",
      "date": "1900"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Legal Recognition in the U.S.",
      "text": "By the mid-20th century, osteopathy gained legal recognition in the United States, with osteopathic physicians (DOs) being granted the same practice rights as allopathic physicians (MDs).",
      "date": "1950"
    },
    {
      "headline": "International Spread",
      "text": "Osteopathy began to spread internationally in the 20th century, with practitioners establishing schools and clinics in Europe, Australia, and other parts of the world.",
      "date": "1950"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Regulation in the United Kingdom",
      "text": "The Osteopaths Act was passed in the United Kingdom in 1993, establishing the General Osteopathic Council to regulate the practice of osteopathy.",
      "date": "1993"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Recognition in Australia",
      "text": "In 2010, osteopathy was recognized as a regulated health profession in Australia under the Australian Health Practitioner Regulation Agency.",
      "date": "2010"
    }
  ]
}

