      "headline": "First European Contact",
      "text": "On October 12, 1492, the Lucayan branch of the Taíno were the first New World peoples encountered by Christopher Columbus in the Bahama Archipelago.",
      "date": "1492-10-12"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Columbus's Second Voyage",
      "text": "In 1493, during his second voyage, Christopher Columbus encountered more Taíno communities in the Greater Antilles, including present-day Puerto Rico and Hispaniola.",
      "date": "1493"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Establishment of Spanish Settlements",
      "text": "By the late 1490s, Spanish settlements began to be established in Taíno territories, leading to significant cultural and demographic changes.",
      "date": "1490"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Taíno Rebellion",
      "text": "In 1511, the Taíno people of Puerto Rico, led by cacique Agüeybaná II, rebelled against Spanish rule, marking one of the first organized resistance efforts by Indigenous peoples in the Caribbean.",
      "date": "1511"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Decline of Taíno Population",
      "text": "By the mid-16th century, the Taíno population had drastically declined due to diseases brought by Europeans, enslavement, and warfare.",
      "date": "1550"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Taíno Cultural Revival",
      "text": "In the late 20th and early 21st centuries, there has been a revival of Taíno culture and identity among descendants and revivalist communities in the Caribbean and beyond.",
      "date": "2000"
    }
  ]
}

