    {
      "headline": "Initial Awareness of Y2K Problem",
      "text": "The potential problem of the Year 2000 (Y2K) issue began to be recognized in the 1980s, as computer scientists and engineers realized that many computer systems represented four-digit years with only the final two digits.",
      "date": "1980"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Increased Awareness and Preparations",
      "text": "Throughout the 1990s, awareness of the Y2K problem increased significantly. Governments and businesses around the world began to assess the potential impact and started preparations to address the issue.",
      "date": "1990"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Y2K Task Forces Established",
      "text": "In the late 1990s, many organizations, including governments and corporations, established Y2K task forces to address the potential impacts of the problem and to ensure systems were compliant before the year 2000.",
      "date": "1997"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Global Y2K Preparations Intensify",
      "text": "By 1999, preparations for the Y2K problem intensified globally. Billions of dollars were spent on remediation efforts, and extensive testing was conducted to ensure systems would function correctly after December 31, 1999.",
      "date": "1999"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Y2K Transition",
      "text": "On January 1, 2000, the world transitioned into the new millennium. Thanks to extensive preparations, the feared widespread failures did not occur, and most systems continued to function correctly.",
      "date": "2000-01-01"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Post-Y2K Analysis",
      "text": "In the years following the transition, analyses were conducted to understand the effectiveness of the Y2K preparations. It was concluded that the extensive efforts and resources dedicated to addressing the issue were largely successful in preventing major disruptions.",
      "date": "2000"
    }
  ]
}

{
  "timeline": [
