      "headline": "Opening of Dachau Concentration Camp",
      "text": "Dachau concentration camp was opened on 22 March 1933. It was one of the first concentration camps established by Nazi Germany and was initially intended to intern Hitler's political opponents, including communists and social democrats.",
      "date": "1933-03-22"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Expansion of Camp's Purpose",
      "text": "After its opening, the purpose of Dachau was enlarged to include forced labor and the imprisonment of Jews, Romani, German and Austrian criminals, and foreign nationals from countries occupied or invaded by Germany.",
      "date": "1933"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Growth of the Dachau Camp System",
      "text": "The Dachau camp system expanded to include nearly 100 sub-camps, mostly work camps or Arbeitskommandos, located throughout southern Germany and Austria.",
      "date": "1933-1945"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Liberation by U.S. Forces",
      "text": "The main camp at Dachau was liberated by U.S. forces on 29 April 1945, marking the end of its operation as a concentration camp.",
      "date": "1945-04-29"
    }
  ]
}

