      "text": "Elagabalus was born as Varius Avitus Bassianus in 204 in Emesa, Syria, into a prominent Syrian Arab family.",
      "date": "0204"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Head Priest of Elagabal",
      "text": "From a young age, Elagabalus served as the head priest of the sun god Elagabal in Emesa, Syria.",
      "date": "0204"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Rise to Power",
      "text": "In 218, at the age of 14, Elagabalus was proclaimed emperor by the Third Legion Gallica after a revolt instigated by his grandmother, Julia Maesa, against the reigning emperor Macrinus.",
      "date": "0218"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Battle of Antioch",
      "text": "On June 8, 218, Elagabalus's forces defeated those of Emperor Macrinus at the Battle of Antioch, solidifying his claim to the throne.",
      "date": "0218-06-08"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Reign as Roman Emperor",
      "text": "Elagabalus reigned as Roman Emperor from 218 to 222. His reign was marked by religious controversy, as he attempted to replace the traditional Roman pantheon with the worship of Elagabal.",
      "date": "0218"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Marriage to Aquilia Severa",
      "text": "Elagabalus married Aquilia Severa, a Vestal Virgin, in 220, causing scandal due to the breaking of her vow of chastity.",
      "date": "0220"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Assassination and Death",
      "text": "Elagabalus was assassinated on March 11, 222, in a plot orchestrated by his grandmother Julia Maesa and the Praetorian Guard. He was killed along with his mother, Julia Soaemias, and their bodies were thrown into the Tiber River.",
      "date": "0222-03-11"
    }
  ]
}

