      "text": "The earliest recorded eruption of Mount Etna occurred in 1500 BCE. This marks the beginning of its long history of volcanic activity.",
      "date": "-1500"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Eruption of 396 BCE",
      "text": "In 396 BCE, an eruption of Mount Etna was said to have prevented the Carthaginian army from advancing on Syracuse during the Second Sicilian War.",
      "date": "-0396"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Eruption of 122 BC",
      "text": "A significant eruption in 122 BC caused extensive damage to the city of Catania, leading to a request for tax relief from the Roman Senate.",
      "date": "-0122"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Eruption of 1669",
      "text": "One of the most destructive eruptions occurred in 1669, when lava flows destroyed at least 10 villages on the southern slope and reached the walls of Catania.",
      "date": "1669"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Eruption of 1928",
      "text": "The 1928 eruption destroyed the town of Mascali, marking the first time in the 20th century that lava flows destroyed a populated area.",
      "date": "1928"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Eruption of 1991-1993",
      "text": "A major eruption from 1991 to 1993 produced a large lava flow that threatened the town of Zafferana Etnea, but was successfully diverted.",
      "date": "1991"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Eruption of 2001",
      "text": "In July 2001, Mount Etna erupted, causing significant disruption to air travel and damaging the tourist station at Rifugio Sapienza.",
      "date": "2001-07"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Eruption of 2002-2003",
      "text": "A series of eruptions from October 2002 to January 2003 caused widespread ash fall and damage to infrastructure, including the Piano Provenzana ski resort.",
      "date": "2002-10"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Eruption of 2017",
      "text": "In March 2017, an eruption injured ten people, including tourists and a BBC news crew, due to phreatomagmatic activity.",
      "date": "2017-03"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Eruption of 2021",
      "text": "Mount Etna experienced a series of paroxysmal eruptions in 2021, with the southeastern crater reaching a height of 3,357 meters.",
      "date": "2021"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Eruption of 2024",
      "text": "In the summer of 2024, eruptions from the Voragine crater increased the height of Mount Etna, surpassing the southeastern crater.",
      "date": "2024"
    }
  ]
}

