      "headline": "Treaty of London",
      "text": "The Treaty of London was signed on April 19, 1839, recognizing Belgium's independence and establishing the borders between Belgium and the Netherlands.",
      "date": "1839-04-19"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Abolition of Slavery in the Dutch Colonies",
      "text": "On July 1, 1863, slavery was abolished in the Dutch colonies, including Suriname and the Dutch Caribbean islands.",
      "date": "1863-07-01"
    },
    {
      "headline": "World War II German Occupation",
      "text": "The Netherlands was occupied by Nazi Germany from May 1940 until its liberation in May 1945 during World War II.",
      "date": "1940-05"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Indonesian Independence",
      "text": "Following a struggle for independence, Indonesia was formally recognized as an independent nation by the Netherlands on December 27, 1949.",
      "date": "1949-12-27"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Suriname Independence",
      "text": "Suriname gained independence from the Kingdom of the Netherlands on November 25, 1975.",
      "date": "1975-11-25"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Dissolution of the Netherlands Antilles",
      "text": "On October 10, 2010, the Netherlands Antilles was dissolved, resulting in the creation of new constituent countries within the Kingdom: Curaçao and Sint Maarten, while Bonaire, Sint Eustatius, and Saba became special municipalities of the Netherlands.",
      "date": "2010-10-10"
    }
  ]
}

{
  "timeline": [
    {
      "headline": "Exploration by Lewis and Clark",
      "text": "The Lewis and Clark Expedition, from 1804 to 1806, was the first American expedition to cross the western portion of the United States. It provided valuable information about the Oregon Country and helped pave the way for future settlers.",
      "date": "1804"
    },
    {
      "headline": "First Use of the Oregon Trail",
      "text": "In 1811, the first known use of the route that would become the Oregon Trail was by fur traders of the Pacific Fur Company, led by Wilson Price Hunt.",
      "date": "1811"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Establishment of the Oregon Trail",
      "text": "The Oregon Trail was established as a major route for emigrants traveling to the western United States in the 1830s. It became a well-traveled path for settlers moving to the Oregon Territory.",
      "date": "1830"
    },
    {
      "headline": "First Large Emigrant Wagon Train",
      "text": "In 1843, the first large wagon train, known as the 'Great Emigration,' traveled the Oregon Trail. Approximately 1,000 emigrants made the journey from Missouri to Oregon.",
      "date": "1843"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Peak of Oregon Trail Migration",
      "text": "The peak period of migration on the Oregon Trail occurred in the mid-1840s to the late 1860s, with thousands of settlers making the journey each year.",
      "date": "1840"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Completion of the Transcontinental Railroad",
      "text": "In 1869, the completion of the First Transcontinental Railroad provided a faster and safer means of travel to the West, leading to a decline in the use of the Oregon Trail.",
      "date": "1869"
    },
    {
      "headline": "End of Significant Use of the Oregon Trail",
      "text": "By the 1870s, the Oregon Trail had largely fallen out of use as the primary route for settlers moving to the western United States, due to the advent of the railroad.",
      "date": "1870"
    }
  ]
}

