      "text": "The Queen of Sheba is first mentioned in the Hebrew Bible, where she visits King Solomon to test his wisdom with hard questions. This event is traditionally dated to the reign of Solomon, which is estimated to be around the 10th century BCE.",
      "date": "-0950"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Visit to King Solomon",
      "text": "According to the biblical account, the Queen of Sheba visits King Solomon in Jerusalem, bringing with her a caravan of camels carrying spices, gold, and precious stones. She tests Solomon with riddles and is impressed by his wisdom and the prosperity of his kingdom. This event is traditionally dated to around the 10th century BCE.",
      "date": "-0950"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Islamic Tradition",
      "text": "In Islamic tradition, the Queen of Sheba is known as Bilqis. The Quran mentions her visit to Solomon and describes her as a ruler who worshipped the sun but converted to monotheism after her encounter with Solomon. This story is part of Islamic teachings and is not dated to a specific year.",
      "date": "-0950"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Ethiopian Tradition",
      "text": "In Ethiopian tradition, the Queen of Sheba is known as Makeda. According to the Kebra Nagast, she visited Solomon and bore him a son, Menelik I, who became the first emperor of Ethiopia. This tradition links the Solomonic dynasty of Ethiopia to the biblical King Solomon.",
      "date": "-0950"
    }
  ]
}

