      "headline": "Miranda v. Arizona Case",
      "text": "The case of Miranda v. Arizona was argued before the U.S. Supreme Court, which would eventually lead to the establishment of the Miranda warning. The case was decided on June 13, 1966.",
      "date": "1966-06-13"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Supreme Court Decision",
      "text": "On June 13, 1966, the U.S. Supreme Court delivered its decision in Miranda v. Arizona, establishing that detained criminal suspects must be informed of their rights to an attorney and against self-incrimination before police questioning.",
      "date": "1966-06-13"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Introduction of the Miranda Warning",
      "text": "Following the Supreme Court decision in 1966, the Miranda warning was introduced as a standard procedure for law enforcement to inform suspects of their rights.",
      "date": "1966"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Yale Kamisar's Influence",
      "text": "Yale Kamisar, a law professor, was recognized for his influential work on the rights of the accused, which contributed to the development of the Miranda warning. He is often referred to as 'the father of Miranda.'",
      "date": "1966"
    }
  ]
}

