      "headline": "Founding of Swallow Sidecar Company",
      "text": "Jaguar Cars was originally founded as the Swallow Sidecar Company in 1922 by William Lyons and William Walmsley. The company initially made motorcycle sidecars before developing bodies for passenger cars.",
      "date": "1922"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Introduction of the SS Jaguar Name",
      "text": "In 1935, the company produced a sports saloon and an open two-seater sports model with the name SS Jaguar.",
      "date": "1935"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Renaming to Jaguar Cars",
      "text": "After World War II, the company was renamed Jaguar Cars in 1945 to avoid the unfavorable connotations of the initials 'SS'.",
      "date": "1945"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Launch of Jaguar XK120",
      "text": "The Jaguar XK120 was launched in 1948 at the London Motor Show as a testbed and show car for the new Jaguar XK engine.",
      "date": "1948"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Introduction of the Jaguar E-Type",
      "text": "The iconic Jaguar E-Type was introduced in 1961, known for its beauty and high performance.",
      "date": "1961"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Merger with British Motor Corporation",
      "text": "Jaguar merged with the British Motor Corporation in 1966 to form British Motor Holdings.",
      "date": "1966"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Formation of British Leyland",
      "text": "In 1968, British Motor Holdings merged with Leyland Motor Corporation to form British Leyland, which was later nationalized.",
      "date": "1968"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Privatization and Independence",
      "text": "Jaguar became an independent company again after being de-merged from British Leyland in 1984.",
      "date": "1984"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Acquisition by Ford",
      "text": "Ford Motor Company acquired Jaguar Cars in 1990, integrating it into its Premier Automotive Group.",
      "date": "1990"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Sale to Tata Motors",
      "text": "In 2008, Ford sold Jaguar Cars, along with Land Rover, to Tata Motors of India.",
      "date": "2008"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Formation of Jaguar Land Rover",
      "text": "On January 1, 2013, Jaguar Cars operations were fully merged with those of Land Rover to form Jaguar Land Rover.",
