      "headline": "Assassination of Julius Caesar",
      "text": "Julius Caesar was assassinated by a group of senators on the Ides of March, March 15, 44 BC, during a meeting of the Senate at the Curia of Pompey of the Theatre of Pompey in Rome. The senators stabbed Caesar 23 times, claiming to act over fears that Caesar's unprecedented concentration of power during his dictatorship was undermining the Roman Republic. At least 60 to 70 senators were involved in the conspiracy, led by Marcus Junius Brutus, Gaius Cassius Longinus, and Decimus Junius Brutus Albinus.",
      "date": "-0044-03-15"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Aftermath of Caesar's Assassination",
      "text": "Despite the death of Caesar, the conspirators were unable to restore the institutions of the Republic. The assassination led to significant ramifications, including Caesar's martyrdom, the Liberators' civil war, and ultimately the transition to the Principate period of the Roman Empire.",
      "date": "-0044"
    }
  ]
}

