      "headline": "Earliest Occurrence of Homo erectus",
      "text": "Homo erectus first appeared around 2 million years ago during the Pleistocene epoch. This species is among the earliest recognizable members of the genus Homo.",
      "date": "-2000000"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Migration Out of Africa",
      "text": "Homo erectus is believed to be the first human ancestor to migrate out of Africa. This migration began approximately 1.9 million years ago, spreading into Asia and Europe.",
      "date": "-1900000"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Use of Fire",
      "text": "Evidence suggests that Homo erectus was among the first to use fire, with evidence dating back to around 1 million years ago. This ability would have been crucial for cooking food and providing warmth.",
      "date": "-1000000"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Acheulean Tool Culture",
      "text": "Homo erectus is associated with the Acheulean tool culture, characterized by distinctive stone handaxes. This culture began around 1.76 million years ago and represents a significant technological advancement.",
      "date": "-1760000"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Presence in Asia",
      "text": "Homo erectus fossils have been found in Asia, with notable sites in Java, Indonesia, and Zhoukoudian, China. These findings date back to approximately 1.8 million years ago.",
      "date": "-1800000"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Extinction of Homo erectus",
      "text": "Homo erectus is believed to have gone extinct around 110,000 years ago, although some populations may have survived until about 50,000 years ago.",
      "date": "-110000"
    }
  ]
}

