      "headline": "First Human Settlement",
      "text": "The first human settlement in the San Francisco Bay Area is believed to have occurred around 3000 BCE by the Ohlone people.",
      "date": "-3000"
    },
    {
      "headline": "European Discovery",
      "text": "The first European to discover the San Francisco Bay was Spanish explorer Gaspar de Portolà in 1769.",
      "date": "1769"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Establishment of Mission San Francisco de Asís",
      "text": "Mission San Francisco de Asís, also known as Mission Dolores, was established by Spanish missionaries in 1776.",
      "date": "1776"
    },
    {
      "headline": "California Gold Rush",
      "text": "The California Gold Rush began in 1848, leading to a massive influx of people to the Bay Area, particularly San Francisco.",
      "date": "1848"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Incorporation of San Francisco",
      "text": "San Francisco was officially incorporated as a city on April 15, 1850.",
      "date": "1850-04-15"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Completion of the Transcontinental Railroad",
      "text": "The First Transcontinental Railroad was completed in 1869, connecting the Bay Area to the rest of the United States.",
      "date": "1869"
    },
    {
      "headline": "San Francisco Earthquake",
      "text": "The 1906 San Francisco earthquake occurred on April 18, causing widespread destruction and loss of life.",
      "date": "1906-04-18"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Golden Gate Bridge Opening",
      "text": "The Golden Gate Bridge opened to vehicular traffic on May 28, 1937, becoming an iconic symbol of the Bay Area.",
      "date": "1937-05-28"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Silicon Valley Emergence",
      "text": "The term 'Silicon Valley' was coined in the early 1970s to describe the region's growing technology industry, centered in the southern part of the Bay Area.",
      "date": "1970"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Loma Prieta Earthquake",
      "text": "The Loma Prieta earthquake struck the Bay Area on October 17, 1989, causing significant damage and loss of life.",
      "date": "1989-10-17"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Dot-com Boom",
      "text": "The late 1990s saw the rise of the dot-com boom, with many internet companies establishing themselves in the Bay Area.",
      "date": "1995"
    },
    {
      "headline": "COVID-19 Pandemic Impact",
      "text": "The COVID-19 pandemic significantly impacted the Bay Area starting in early 2020, leading to lockdowns and changes in work and lifestyle.",
      "date": "2020"
    }
  ]
}

{
  "timeline": [
