      "headline": "First Known Laws on Slavery",
      "text": "The Code of Hammurabi, dating back to around 1754 BCE, is one of the earliest known sets of laws, which included regulations on slavery.",
      "date": "-1754"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Slavery in Ancient Greece",
      "text": "Slavery was a common practice in Ancient Greece, with slaves being used for various forms of labor. The practice was well-established by the 5th century BCE.",
      "date": "-0500"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Roman Empire and Slavery",
      "text": "Slavery was integral to the Roman economy and society. By the 1st century CE, slaves were used in households, agriculture, mines, and other sectors.",
      "date": "0001"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Islamic Golden Age and Slavery",
      "text": "During the Islamic Golden Age (8th to 13th centuries), slavery was prevalent in the Islamic world, with slaves being used in various capacities.",
      "date": "0800"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Transatlantic Slave Trade Begins",
      "text": "The transatlantic slave trade began in the early 16th century, with European powers transporting African slaves to the Americas.",
      "date": "1500"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Abolition of the Slave Trade Act",
      "text": "The British Parliament passed the Abolition of the Slave Trade Act on March 25, 1807, making it illegal to engage in the slave trade throughout the British Empire.",
      "date": "1807-03-25"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Emancipation Proclamation",
      "text": "On January 1, 1863, U.S. President Abraham Lincoln issued the Emancipation Proclamation, declaring all slaves in Confederate-held territory to be free.",
      "date": "1863-01-01"
    },
    {
      "headline": "13th Amendment to the U.S. Constitution",
      "text": "The 13th Amendment, which abolished slavery in the United States, was ratified on December 6, 1865.",
      "date": "1865-12-06"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Slavery Abolished in Brazil",
      "text": "On May 13, 1888, Brazil abolished slavery with the signing of the Lei Áurea (Golden Law) by Princess Isabel.",
      "date": "1888-05-13"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Slavery Convention",
      "text": "The League of Nations adopted the Slavery Convention on September 25, 1926, aiming to eradicate slavery worldwide.",
      "date": "1926-09-25"
    }
  ]
}

