      "headline": "Design and Development",
      "text": "The AK-74 was designed by Mikhail Kalashnikov in 1974 as a successor to the AKM. It was developed to use the new 5.45×39mm cartridge, which offered improved performance over the previous 7.62×39mm cartridge.",
      "date": "1974"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Introduction to Soviet Armed Forces",
      "text": "The AK-74 was officially adopted by the Soviet Armed Forces in 1974, marking a significant shift in the standard infantry weaponry of the Soviet military.",
      "date": "1974"
    },
    {
      "headline": "First Combat Use",
      "text": "The AK-74 saw its first combat use during the Soviet-Afghan War, which began in December 1979. It was used extensively by Soviet troops throughout the conflict.",
      "date": "1979-12"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Widespread Adoption",
      "text": "Throughout the 1980s, the AK-74 was widely adopted by Warsaw Pact countries and other Soviet allies, becoming a standard issue rifle in many Eastern Bloc nations.",
      "date": "1980"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Modernization and Variants",
      "text": "In the years following its introduction, several variants of the AK-74 were developed, including the AKS-74 with a folding stock, the AK-74M with modernized features, and the RPK-74 light machine gun variant.",
      "date": "1980"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Post-Soviet Use",
      "text": "After the dissolution of the Soviet Union in 1991, the AK-74 continued to be used by the Russian military and other former Soviet states. It remains in service in various forms to this day.",
      "date": "1991"
    }
  ]
}

