      "text": "Following the Norman Conquest in 1066, the system of counties was further developed and formalized.",
      "date": "1066"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Creation of Ceremonial Counties",
      "text": "The ceremonial counties, also known as geographic counties, were established for the purposes of lieutenancy. These counties are used for the appointment of Lord-Lieutenants, the monarch's representatives.",
      "date": "1889"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Local Government Act 1888",
      "text": "The Local Government Act 1888 created county councils and redefined the boundaries of administrative counties.",
      "date": "1888"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Local Government Act 1972",
      "text": "The Local Government Act 1972 reorganized the administrative counties and created metropolitan and non-metropolitan counties, which came into effect in 1974.",
      "date": "1974"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Abolition of Metropolitan County Councils",
      "text": "In 1986, the metropolitan county councils were abolished, although the counties themselves remained as geographic and ceremonial entities.",
      "date": "1986"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Introduction of Unitary Authorities",
      "text": "Since the 1990s, several counties have been reorganized to create unitary authorities, which combine the functions of county and district councils.",
      "date": "1990"
    }
  ]
