      "headline": "Debris Recovery",
      "text": "In early July 1947, a rancher named Mac Brazel discovered unusual debris on his property near Roswell, New Mexico. The debris was later collected by personnel from the Roswell Army Air Field.",
      "date": "1947-07"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Initial Army Announcement",
      "text": "On July 8, 1947, the United States Army announced that they had recovered a 'flying disc' from the debris found near Roswell. This announcement quickly made international headlines.",
      "date": "1947-07-08"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Retraction and Weather Balloon Explanation",
      "text": "On July 9, 1947, the Army retracted their initial statement, claiming instead that the debris was from a conventional weather balloon, not a 'flying disc'. This was done to obscure the true purpose of the debris, which was part of the top secret Project Mogul.",
      "date": "1947-07-09"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Project Mogul Revealed",
      "text": "Project Mogul, the top secret project responsible for the balloon debris, was intended to detect Soviet nuclear tests. The true nature of the project was not publicly revealed until decades later.",
      "date": "1994"
    }
  ]
}

