      "headline": "Indian Removal Act Signed",
      "text": "On May 28, 1830, President Andrew Jackson signed the Indian Removal Act into law, authorizing the forced relocation of Native American tribes living east of the Mississippi River to lands west of the river.",
      "date": "1830-05-28"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Treaty of Dancing Rabbit Creek",
      "text": "In September 1830, the Treaty of Dancing Rabbit Creek was signed, ceding Choctaw lands in Mississippi to the United States and beginning the process of their removal.",
      "date": "1830-09"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Treaty of New Echota",
      "text": "On December 29, 1835, the Treaty of New Echota was signed by a small faction of the Cherokee Nation, ceding all Cherokee land east of the Mississippi River to the United States in exchange for compensation.",
      "date": "1835-12-29"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Cherokee Removal Begins",
      "text": "In 1838, the forced removal of the Cherokee people, known as the Trail of Tears, began. Thousands of Cherokee were forcibly relocated from their ancestral lands in the southeastern United States to Indian Territory in present-day Oklahoma.",
      "date": "1838"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Seminole Resistance and Removal",
      "text": "Between 1835 and 1842, the Second Seminole War took place as the Seminole resisted removal from Florida. Despite their resistance, many Seminole were eventually removed to Indian Territory.",
      "date": "1835-1842"
    },
    {
      "headline": "End of the Trail of Tears",
      "text": "By 1850, the forced relocations known as the Trail of Tears were largely complete, with thousands of Native Americans having been displaced from their homelands.",
      "date": "1850"
    }
  ]
}

