    {
      "headline": "Foundation of Tenochtitlan",
      "text": "According to Aztec legend, the city of Tenochtitlan was founded on March 13, 1325. The Mexica people, guided by their god Huitzilopochtli, settled on an island in Lake Texcoco where they saw an eagle perched on a cactus, eating a snake.",
      "date": "1325-03-13"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Formation of the Aztec Triple Alliance",
      "text": "In 1427, the Mexica of Tenochtitlan formed a military alliance with the city-states of Tetzcoco and Tlacopan, creating the Aztec Triple Alliance. This alliance marked the beginning of the Aztec Empire's expansion.",
      "date": "1427"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Reign of Moctezuma I",
      "text": "Moctezuma I, also known as Moctezuma Ilhuicamina, ruled the Aztec Empire from 1440 to 1469. During his reign, the empire expanded significantly, and he implemented important social, political, and religious reforms.",
      "date": "1440"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Construction of the Great Temple of Tenochtitlan",
      "text": "The Great Temple, or Templo Mayor, was completed in 1487 under the rule of Ahuitzotl. It was dedicated to the gods Huitzilopochtli and Tlaloc and became the religious center of the Aztec Empire.",
      "date": "1487"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Reign of Moctezuma II",
      "text": "Moctezuma II, also known as Moctezuma Xocoyotzin, ruled from 1502 to 1520. His reign was marked by the empire's greatest territorial expansion, but also by the arrival of Spanish conquistadors.",
      "date": "1502"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Spanish Conquest of the Aztec Empire",
      "text": "In 1519, Hernán Cortés and his Spanish forces arrived in the Aztec Empire. By August 13, 1521, after a series of battles and alliances with indigenous groups, the Spanish had captured Tenochtitlan, marking the fall of the Aztec Empire.",
      "date": "1521-08-13"
    }
  ]
}

