    {
      "headline": "First Use of the Term 'Steampunk'",
      "text": "The term 'steampunk' was first coined by science fiction author K.W. Jeter in a letter to the science fiction magazine Locus, published in April 1987. Jeter used the term to describe a genre of speculative fiction that he, along with Tim Powers and James Blaylock, were writing, which was set in an alternate Victorian era.",
      "date": "1987-04"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Publication of 'The Difference Engine'",
      "text": "In 1990, William Gibson and Bruce Sterling published 'The Difference Engine', a novel that is often credited with popularizing the steampunk genre. The book imagines an alternate history where Charles Babbage's analytical engine was built and led to the development of advanced steam-powered technology.",
      "date": "1990"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Steampunk Enters Popular Culture",
      "text": "Throughout the 1990s and 2000s, steampunk began to gain popularity in various forms of media, including literature, film, and fashion. This period saw the release of numerous steampunk-themed books, movies, and TV shows, contributing to the genre's growing cultural presence.",
      "date": "1990s-2000s"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Steampunk Fashion and Subculture",
      "text": "By the early 2000s, steampunk had evolved into a distinct subculture, characterized by a unique fashion style that combines Victorian-era clothing with modern and fantastical elements. Steampunk conventions and gatherings became popular, celebrating the aesthetic and community.",
      "date": "2000s"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Steampunk in Video Games",
      "text": "The 2000s and 2010s saw the rise of steampunk-themed video games, such as 'Bioshock Infinite' (2013) and 'Dishonored' (2012), which helped to further popularize the genre and introduce it to new audiences.",
      "date": "2010s"
    }
  ]
}

